<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=208121.
-->
<Project ToolsVersion="4.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PublishProtocol>FileSystem</PublishProtocol>
    <Platform>x64</Platform>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <PublishDir>bin\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)\$(RuntimeIdentifier)\publish\</PublishDir>
    <SelfContained>true</SelfContained>
    <PublishSingleFile>False</PublishSingleFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    What belongs in this file, and why the two packaging switches in docs/packaging.md §3 are on the
    command line rather than in here. Measured on 2026-09-11.

    O-20260911-09, which asked for the move, opens on a premise that is not true of this tree and is
    corrected here rather than repeated: it says `RuntimeIdentifier`, `Platform` and `PublishProfile`
    are each declared in two places. Only `RuntimeIdentifier` is - csproj line 14 and line 9 above.
    The csproj declares `<Platforms>` (plural, the list a project may be built for), which is a
    different property from this file's `<Platform>` (singular, the one being built); and
    `<PublishProfile>` is declared in the csproj alone and appears nowhere here. So there was no
    precedence question to settle. What there is, is a placement rule, and it is the one below.

    **This file is read by an ordinary `dotnet build`, not only by `dotnet publish`.** The csproj
    sets `<PublishProfile>win-x64.pubxml</PublishProfile>`, and the SDK imports the named profile
    during evaluation - so every property in here is in force for every build of this project. That
    is what makes the two switches wrong here, and both were tried:

    - `<GenerateAppxPackageOnBuild>true</GenerateAppxPackageOnBuild>` breaks `dotnet build`
      outright: APPX0101, *a signing key is required in order to package this project*, with an
      `AppPackages\MeetingTranscriber.App_1.0.0.0_x64_Debug_Test\` folder left behind - because a
      plain build then tries to package, and no certificate is on that line.
    - `<AppxPackageSigningEnabled>true</AppxPackageSigningEnabled>` on its own leaves `dotnet build`
      green but warning `SigningCertificateNoCertSpecified`, which CI's `-warnaserror` turns into a
      failed build.

    So the rule is: a property belongs here when it is true of every build of this project, and on
    the command line when it is true only of the packaging one. What goes inside the layout - the
    RID, self-contained, not single-file - is the first kind. Producing an `.msix` and signing it is
    the second, and so is the certificate, which is a fact about the machine typing it.

    What that leaves standing is the hazard those two switches are: easy to forget, and silent when
    they are. They are no longer silent one command later, which is the half of this that could be
    had - `PackagedAppTests` under `MEETING_TRANSCRIBER_EXPECT_PACKAGE` fails when there is no
    package at all, and `The_package_is_signed` fails on a package carrying no `AppxSignature.p7x`.
  -->
</Project>